1
00:00:24,027 --> 00:00:29,393
Hello and welcome to the NPTEL MOOCs course on Foundation for Virtual and Augmented Reality Systems.

2
00:00:31,175 --> 00:00:36,521
In this lecture, we are going to start discussion on a new topic, that is Requirement Gathering.

3
00:00:37,583 --> 00:00:53,781
Now, previously we talked about extended reality systems as examples of interactive systems, and the main concern in interactive system development is the

4
00:00:54,757 --> 00:00:57,380
taking care of the concern of usability.

5
00:00:58,301 --> 00:01:07,491
Now, in this lecture, what we are going to do is we shall learn about the methods for gathering end user requirements so that usability is ensured.

6
00:01:08,953 --> 00:01:18,283
So, what are the processes that we can follow to gather end user requirements which will lead to a usable design that we are going to learn in this lecture?

7
00:01:19,883 --> 00:01:25,791
Now, we shall first go through the general idea about user requirement gathering and also specification.

8
00:01:26,012 --> 00:01:28,395
Specification is required for system implementation.

9
00:01:28,475 --> 00:01:37,107
So, we will learn in general what are the techniques used to gather the requirements and also specify them.

10
00:01:39,210 --> 00:01:45,098
Then we shall link our discussion, our theoretical knowledge,

11
00:01:45,601 --> 00:01:56,072
with respect to the case study that we have introduced earlier, that is we shall talk about the specific case of Majuli VR Tour requirement gathering and specification.

12
00:01:58,274 --> 00:02:02,458
So, let us first learn about usability requirements, what it means.

13
00:02:03,099 --> 00:02:13,009
Now, when we talk of requirement gathering, so let us quickly recollect the interactive system development life cycle as we have learned about it in the previous lecture.

14
00:02:13,394 --> 00:02:24,908
So, this is the life cycle; it comprises a feasibility study, requirement gathering, analysis and specification, then a design prototype evaluation cycle,

15
00:02:24,908 --> 00:02:37,987
code design, actual coding, then code testing, then an empirical study or usability testing, and finally deployment.

16
00:02:39,268 --> 00:02:42,793
So, we are going to learn about this stage, requirement gathering stage.

17
00:02:43,313 --> 00:02:49,499
With respect to end user requirements, not the client requirements.

18
00:02:49,519 --> 00:02:53,664
Now, we introduce the term system user-centered design.

19
00:02:53,684 --> 00:02:54,504
So, there are two things.

20
00:02:54,645 --> 00:03:01,451
One is system-centered design; the other one is user-centered or user-centric or human-centric design.

21
00:03:01,471 --> 00:03:04,314
One is SCD; the other one we can refer to as UCD.

22
00:03:05,796 --> 00:03:08,639
So, what is the difference between the two?

23
00:03:08,659 --> 00:03:11,802
Let us try to understand with respect to some

24
00:03:13,216 --> 00:03:18,606
interesting comic strips.

25
00:03:18,626 --> 00:03:19,908
Consider these two characters.

26
00:03:20,149 --> 00:03:20,930
They are conversing.

27
00:03:21,111 --> 00:03:23,615
One is the software developer; the other one is the manager.

28
00:03:24,677 --> 00:03:27,703
This is a Dilbert strip.

29
00:03:27,723 --> 00:03:31,770
Some of you may have been reading it regularly.

30
00:03:33,373 --> 00:03:35,477
So the developer talks about

31
00:03:36,098 --> 00:03:43,808
how to better develop the system: we could design the product with a simple point-and-click interface so that users find it easy to operate.

32
00:03:46,331 --> 00:03:54,261
So, the developer is talking about all the possible options, or we could require the user to choose among thousands of poorly documented commands,

33
00:03:54,261 --> 00:04:00,550
each of which must be typed exactly right on the first try; otherwise, the system will crash or hang.

34
00:04:03,274 --> 00:04:05,937
So interestingly, the developer

35
00:04:06,490 --> 00:04:12,879
discusses all possible design options or development options with the manager.

36
00:04:16,223 --> 00:04:27,319
In continuation of those earlier options, the developer also talks about, bear in mind, we will never meet a customer ourselves because they are the developers, customers are hidden from them.

37
00:04:28,821 --> 00:04:33,527
And the manager advises that make it so they have to reboot after every typo.

38
00:04:36,022 --> 00:04:44,951
Now, this sequence of events, although if a system is designed in such a bad way, intuitively we feel that it is a very bad system.

39
00:04:45,412 --> 00:04:48,014
However, that is not necessarily the case always.

40
00:04:48,575 --> 00:05:01,708
When we are talking of system-centered design, that is, we are only bothered about the system rather than the user of the system, then probably this type of approach makes sense.

41
00:05:03,899 --> 00:05:09,927
So, the main concerns for system-centered designers: what can I easily build on this platform?

42
00:05:10,147 --> 00:05:12,930
I means the developer or the manager.

43
00:05:14,192 --> 00:05:16,154
What can I create from the available tools?

44
00:05:16,254 --> 00:05:17,856
Again, I is the developer.

45
00:05:19,118 --> 00:05:21,601
What do I as a programmer find interesting?

46
00:05:23,123 --> 00:05:24,845
So, here I refers to the developer.

47
00:05:27,929 --> 00:05:33,636
So, essentially, the development is driven by the skill and

48
00:05:34,510 --> 00:05:40,777
interest of the developer rather than the needs and expectations of the user.

49
00:05:41,037 --> 00:05:43,260
So, that is the system-centered design.

50
00:05:44,901 --> 00:05:48,625
On the other hand, we are concerned here about user-centered design.

51
00:05:49,827 --> 00:06:00,298
In such a case, the design is based on a user's abilities, context, goals, needs, expectations, and such things.

52
00:06:01,105 --> 00:06:08,397
So, the concern here is not the programmer's convenience; it is about the user's abilities, user's goals, user's needs.

53
00:06:10,621 --> 00:06:11,723
So, the viewpoint changes.

54
00:06:12,784 --> 00:06:18,224
So, in system-centered design, the entire thing is driven by the developer's point of view,

55
00:06:18,224 --> 00:06:25,105
whereas in user-centered design, ideally everything should be driven by the users, from the user's point of view.

56
00:06:29,945 --> 00:06:32,809
Let us try to understand these in terms of one example.

57
00:06:32,909 --> 00:06:38,357
So, in the previous lecture, we introduced the calendar application, a mobile application.

58
00:06:40,501 --> 00:06:53,680
Now, when we talk of requirement gathering for the calendar application, when we know that the calendar application has to be developed following a user-centered approach, design approach.

59
00:06:55,643 --> 00:06:57,946
So, we have to gather the requirements.

60
00:06:58,427 --> 00:06:59,749
What are the requirements?

61
00:07:00,117 --> 00:07:05,725
If we are asked to gather the end user requirements, what potentially can be such requirements?

62
00:07:09,130 --> 00:07:15,440
If we say we need to develop it for the mobile platform, is it part of the requirement?

63
00:07:17,723 --> 00:07:25,875
If we say what programming language we are going to use, say for example Java or JavaScript, is it a requirement?

64
00:07:28,259 --> 00:07:28,960
If we say

65
00:07:29,834 --> 00:07:35,239
How are we going to store the various information needed, such as reminders and special events?

66
00:07:37,401 --> 00:07:41,305
Should we use some RDBMS tools such as MySQL?

67
00:07:42,926 --> 00:07:43,787
Is it a requirement?

68
00:07:47,250 --> 00:07:50,994
Are these basically end user requirements which ensure usability?

69
00:07:53,757 --> 00:07:57,360
There may be many more such questions which may come to

70
00:07:59,686 --> 00:08:06,874
come to the developer's mind regarding the requirements.

71
00:08:09,476 --> 00:08:14,221
But these are not requirements as such.

72
00:08:14,842 --> 00:08:21,069
In fact, here the term requirement specifically refers to end user requirements.

73
00:08:21,089 --> 00:08:24,092
So, these are not essentially end user requirements as such.

74
00:08:25,593 --> 00:08:28,056
Now, some of these requirements are related to feasibility.

75
00:08:28,930 --> 00:08:36,256
the first stage, such as we need to develop it for mobile platforms, what programming languages are we going to use.

76
00:08:36,537 --> 00:08:40,140
These are requirements related to feasibility.

77
00:08:40,159 --> 00:08:42,182
Some may be related to the design stage.

78
00:08:43,283 --> 00:08:48,548
Remember that we have utilized the notion of design prototype evaluate cycle.

79
00:08:49,909 --> 00:08:55,894
So, some may be related to the design state of the interface or design stage of the code.

80
00:08:55,914 --> 00:08:56,575
Now, here.

81
00:08:58,597 --> 00:09:05,803
When we have questions like, how are we going to store the various information needed, such as reminders or special events,

82
00:09:05,803 --> 00:09:14,903
should we use some RDBMS tools such as MySQL? These are part of the code design stage, not the interface design.

83
00:09:20,992 --> 00:09:25,920
So, the questions that may come to our mind are:

84
00:09:26,643 --> 00:09:31,031
They are not necessarily requirements, but they are part of other stages.

85
00:09:32,113 --> 00:09:42,774
So, our objective is to identify requirements that ensure usability and lead to system specification.

86
00:09:42,814 --> 00:09:43,675
So, both are important.

87
00:09:43,776 --> 00:09:48,685
One is we have to identify requirements that ensure usability, but

88
00:09:49,087 --> 00:09:58,139
Also, we need to be able to specify those requirements so that they can be converted to plans for implementing the system and deploying the system.

89
00:09:58,740 --> 00:10:04,488
So, system specification is also important for design and implementation of the system.

90
00:10:08,273 --> 00:10:08,974
So, what do we need?

91
00:10:09,836 --> 00:10:15,904
Gathering of usability requirements and analysis and specification of the requirements to

92
00:10:16,576 --> 00:10:21,283
be able to convert those to system design and subsequently implementation.

93
00:10:25,469 --> 00:10:37,127
Now, when we talk of usability requirements or gathering of usability requirements, such requirements are generally treated as a type of non-functional requirements.

94
00:10:38,909 --> 00:10:44,878
So, those of you who have done a course on software engineering, you know that.

95
00:10:45,854 --> 00:10:48,938
There are two types of requirements: functional and non-functional.

96
00:10:50,601 --> 00:10:53,385
Here, we are dealing with non-functional requirements.

97
00:10:54,206 --> 00:10:59,514
Usability requirements are essentially part of non-functional requirements.

98
00:10:59,534 --> 00:11:04,881
Now, let us quickly have some idea about this non-functional requirement.

99
00:11:05,362 --> 00:11:11,050
So, these are system characteristics that cannot be expressed as functions.

100
00:11:11,070 --> 00:11:14,275
So, when we talk about functional requirements, essentially we are referring to

101
00:11:16,330 --> 00:11:25,402
system features and abilities of the system to carry out tasks in terms of some functions which are treated as black boxes.

102
00:11:25,422 --> 00:11:30,930
So, we provide some input and the function generates some output once it is implemented.

103
00:11:32,252 --> 00:11:39,462
Those are functional requirements.

104
00:11:39,482 --> 00:11:45,510
As opposed to that, when we are talking about usability requirements, these are system characteristics

105
00:11:46,165 --> 00:11:50,892
along with many other such characteristics which cannot be expressed in terms of functions.

106
00:11:51,914 --> 00:11:55,519
They are typically related to quality attributes of the software.

107
00:11:58,523 --> 00:12:05,400
Now, there are several categories of such non-functional requirements, such as performance-related non-functional requirements,

108
00:12:05,400 --> 00:12:12,544
operating constraints as non-functional requirements, economic considerations.

109
00:12:13,030 --> 00:12:19,416
lifecycle requirements, as well as interface issues.

110
00:12:19,436 --> 00:12:24,201
Now, our interest lies in this last category: interface issues.

111
00:12:24,221 --> 00:12:37,074
So, when we talk of interface issues, essentially it refers to the design of the interface or the qualities of the interface that should be usable, that should make the interface usable.

112
00:12:39,917 --> 00:12:42,379
Now, in fact, there are two issues.

113
00:12:43,085 --> 00:12:47,609
One is how the system is to interface with its environment, users, and other systems.

114
00:12:48,290 --> 00:12:52,614
All these concerns are taken care of under the interface issues.

115
00:12:52,634 --> 00:12:57,796
Now, it has an external system interface; that means hardware and software interfacing,

116
00:12:57,796 --> 00:13:08,488
interfacing between the hardware and software, as well as the user interface where usability issues come into the picture.

117
00:13:08,929 --> 00:13:10,510
Now usability is our focus.

118
00:13:11,637 --> 00:13:16,802
So, external system interface we are not going to talk about much in this course.

119
00:13:21,027 --> 00:13:36,483
Now, when we talk of usability, we have to remember that we are dealing with interactive systems meant to be used by non-experts or those who are not aware of how the system works.

120
00:13:38,265 --> 00:13:41,188
So, in that context usability is very important.

121
00:13:44,122 --> 00:13:56,453
Now with that knowledge, let us now try to understand the requirement gathering methods that we can deploy to gather requirements and also ensure usability.

122
00:14:00,076 --> 00:14:07,663
So when we are talking of gathering end user requirements, our motto should be know the user.

123
00:14:08,083 --> 00:14:12,087
In other words, know the user, who are the users of your system.

124
00:14:12,370 --> 00:14:22,184
So, that should be our very first step unless we are able to categorize the users unless we are able to specify the users we will not be able to gather end user requirements.

125
00:14:25,469 --> 00:14:32,539
Now, in this context let us quickly recap the usability definition which we have encountered earlier.

126
00:14:34,141 --> 00:14:40,690
The ISO definition of usability, which we have encountered earlier, is the extent to which

127
00:14:41,159 --> 00:14:55,534
A product can be used by specified users to achieve specified goals with effectiveness, efficiency, and satisfaction in a specified context of use.

128
00:14:55,554 --> 00:15:08,247
So, it talks about a usable product with respect to a specified group of users trying to achieve specified goals in a specified context of use.

129
00:15:08,716 --> 00:15:12,201
with effectiveness, efficiency, and satisfaction.

130
00:15:13,683 --> 00:15:20,232
So, essentially, any usable product must have a specified context of use.

131
00:15:21,353 --> 00:15:34,591
Now, based on that idea, we can talk about one requirement gathering method, an end user requirement gathering method that is called contextual inquiry.

132
00:15:35,953 --> 00:15:37,255
Note that there can be many

133
00:15:38,028 --> 00:15:39,249
requirement gathering methods.

134
00:15:40,150 --> 00:15:54,446
One is contextual inquiry, which is a method to gather end user requirements which in our context is the same as usability requirements, and one of many such methods.

135
00:15:54,907 --> 00:15:58,711
So, do not assume that this is the only method possible; there are many such methods.

136
00:15:58,811 --> 00:16:05,959
However, we will be going through this method in a little more detailed manner.

137
00:16:06,412 --> 00:16:14,144
So, as to understand how the usability requirements are gathered and subsequently how those are used, analyzed, and used.

138
00:16:17,189 --> 00:16:20,435
Now, the contextual inquiry method is an old method.

139
00:16:20,955 --> 00:16:31,913
Its importance in user-centered design was highlighted way back in 1988, although the term used at that time was phenomenological research method.

140
00:16:32,753 --> 00:16:37,238
And by the end of the 1990s, it became well developed and popular.

141
00:16:37,858 --> 00:16:39,720
So, it is more than 30 years old.

142
00:16:43,984 --> 00:16:52,473
Now, this method is often referred to as a semi-structured interview process, which consists of both interviews and observations.

143
00:16:56,417 --> 00:17:01,242
Now, what is the primary objective of this method of contextual inquiry?

144
00:17:01,778 --> 00:17:13,313
The primary objective is to watch and observe users perform tasks in their natural work setting or, in other words, the work context.

145
00:17:14,694 --> 00:17:28,291
So, when you say usability is the quality when it is measured against a specified context of use, in other words, the work context.

146
00:17:30,112 --> 00:17:41,770
So, contextual inquiry essentially tries to gather end user requirements when the users are working in a specified context, which we can call a natural work setting.

147
00:17:42,431 --> 00:17:53,748
Now, during observations, when we are following the contextual inquiry method, the users may be asked questions to clarify certain behaviors or activities.

148
00:17:55,991 --> 00:17:58,515
So, this is the part of the interview.

149
00:17:59,035 --> 00:18:00,817
That is asking for clarification.

150
00:18:01,338 --> 00:18:08,087
However, the primary objective is to observe and to gather more insight about user behavior.

151
00:18:08,968 --> 00:18:14,335
The method also provides for interacting with the users for clarification.

152
00:18:18,120 --> 00:18:22,866
Generally, it takes one to two hours if everything is well planned.

153
00:18:27,740 --> 00:18:39,936
Now, the observations including interaction with the users are supposed to enable the designers to learn about the task domain,

154
00:18:39,936 --> 00:18:46,482
work culture and physical and social constraints at the workplace based on which design decisions can be made.

155
00:18:48,945 --> 00:18:55,553
So, the knowledge is important since the specific work context is an essential part of the definition of usability.

156
00:18:56,444 --> 00:19:01,542
So, since usability is defined in a specific work context,

157
00:19:01,542 --> 00:19:13,971
it is essential to know how the users behave in that specific work context to ensure that the software or the product or the system that we are building is usable.

158
00:19:18,398 --> 00:19:23,346
Let us try to understand the importance of this method,

159
00:19:23,647 --> 00:19:25,870
From the point of view of system development.

160
00:19:25,991 --> 00:19:35,225
For example, let us recollect the calendar app that we have already talked about.

161
00:19:35,245 --> 00:19:42,857
Let us assume that the target group of users for the app are the managerial staff of any organization.

162
00:19:42,877 --> 00:19:48,405
Remember that we have to start by first identifying the group, the user group.

163
00:19:48,425 --> 00:19:50,869
So, in this case, let us assume that the user group

164
00:19:51,592 --> 00:19:59,020
comprises the managerial staff of a given organization.

165
00:19:59,040 --> 00:20:10,211
Now, without any knowledge about how the managerial staff behave in general when they are in their work setting, we can come up with all sorts of possible designs.

166
00:20:10,552 --> 00:20:20,282
So, we do not know what the unique behavioral traits of the managerial staff are unless we study them.

167
00:20:21,258 --> 00:20:29,307
And unless we study them, unless we gather the knowledge, we will not be able to refine the design.

168
00:20:29,508 --> 00:20:38,538
We will only be coming up with all sorts of design possibilities, although some of those or the majority of those may not be valid in the given work context.

169
00:20:41,081 --> 00:20:49,611
However, let us assume that we have performed a contextual inquiry and in the inquiry method, we observed

170
00:20:50,755 --> 00:20:55,801
Two important activities performed by the users, that is the managerial staff.

171
00:20:56,682 --> 00:20:59,826
Set meeting dates and reminders.

172
00:21:04,131 --> 00:21:12,402
Now, with this knowledge, we can be certain that these features must be included in the design.

173
00:21:12,462 --> 00:21:16,847
Otherwise, the system will not be usable for the given user group.

174
00:21:17,620 --> 00:21:25,289
So clearly, a calendar app intended to be used by this group of users must have support for these activities.

175
00:21:26,110 --> 00:21:31,838
Now if we had not done the contextual inquiry this knowledge would not have come to us and

176
00:21:31,838 --> 00:21:43,491
we probably would have come up with some design having all sorts of features where these two are more important but are given equal importance with other unnecessary features.

177
00:21:44,512 --> 00:21:45,433
So in that case,

178
00:21:47,253 --> 00:21:49,236
the usability would have been compromised.

179
00:21:50,457 --> 00:21:53,061
So, that is the importance of contextual inquiry.

180
00:21:55,625 --> 00:21:59,170
So, let us summarize what we have learned so far.

181
00:22:00,972 --> 00:22:04,617
We started talking about usability requirement gathering.

182
00:22:08,262 --> 00:22:15,252
That is a very essential part of ensuring a usable design; unless we gather the usability requirements,

183
00:22:16,784 --> 00:22:23,635
we will not be able to come up with a proper design that takes care of users' needs and expectations.

184
00:22:24,416 --> 00:22:26,419
Now, there are different methods to do that.

185
00:22:26,920 --> 00:22:29,504
So, we are talking about one method: contextual inquiry.

186
00:22:31,507 --> 00:22:34,432
We started with the basic idea of the method.

187
00:22:35,033 --> 00:22:43,686
In the next part of the lecture, we are going to continue on this method of requirement gathering.

188
00:22:45,067 --> 00:22:48,315
So, I hope you are able to follow whatever we have covered.

189
00:22:49,057 --> 00:22:51,082
So, we will continue in the next part of the lecture.

190
00:22:51,924 --> 00:22:54,410
Looking forward to meeting you all again.

191
00:22:55,493 --> 00:22:57,658
So, thank you, goodbye, and happy learning.
